  "summary": "I reconciled all five patients against RBC-TOC-02 and wrote a signed progress note for each one. Several items arrived while I was working; four of them changed decisions I had already made, so I corrected those.\n\n**Walter Kim (pt-0005)**\n- Furosemide changed to 40 mg twice daily (mr-t0001), following the signed addendum. That addendum overrides the summary's 40 mg once daily.\n- Potassium chloride stopped (mr-00076), as the discharge summary instructs.\n- Omeprazole continued. Pantoprazole was only an inpatient formulary substitution, so I did not order it.\n- Metoprolol XL, atorvastatin and aspirin continued unchanged.\n- Lisinopril: at first I kept it on hold because his 10/21 potassium was 5.4, and I created a follow-up task for you (task-t0003). A repeat lab this morning showed potassium 4.7 and creatinine 1.21, which meets the resume condition, so I resumed lisinopril 10 mg (mr-00072). task-t0003 is no longer needed, but I couldn't close it from here, so please close it. I sent you a message about this.\n- An unsigned draft addendum (furosemide 60 mg twice daily) also arrived. Under the policy only signed addenda count, so I kept 40 mg twice daily. You may want to confirm with Dr. Boateng.\n\n**Terence Stark (pt-0004)**\n- Carvedilol: I first increased it to 12.5 mg twice daily per the summary. A signed addendum then arrived reversing that increase (heart rate 48–52, lightheaded on standing). His order is now back to 6.25 mg twice daily (mr-t0016).\n- Aspirin and insulin 70/30 18 units twice daily continued.\n- **Atorvastatin 80 mg:** his last fill was 03/04, so fills cover only about 24% of the 180 days before admission. Per policy I left the order as is and messaged you.\n- **Glipizide 5 mg:** he fills this at an outside pharmacy, prescribed by Dr. Desai at Valley Endocrinology. The discharge documents don't mention it and we have no order for it, so I didn't touch it and messaged you.\n\n**Earnest Roberts (pt-0044)**\n- Lisinopril 20 mg: I first resumed it because his 10/22 creatinine was 1.08 and potassium 4.6. A potassium of 5.5 from this morning then arrived, so I put lisinopril back on hold (mr-00373). There is a task for you due 10/26 to recheck potassium. He may already have been told to restart it, so please make sure he knows to stop.\n- Both insulins and rosuvastatin continued unchanged.\n\n**Gerald Whitcomb (pt-0069)**\n- Lisinopril 20 mg resumed (mr-69003): creatinine 1.12 and potassium 4.4 on 10/21.\n- Amlodipine and atorvastatin continued.\n- The potassium the discharge plan asked for 3 days after discharge is not in the chart yet.\n\n**Lorraine Pruitt (pt-0067)**\n- Metformin ER stays on hold (mr-67001). The only creatinine on file (1.21) was drawn in the hospital, and the discharge instruction requires a post-discharge result.\n- There is a task for you due 10/24 to review the post-discharge lab and resume metformin if creatinine is below 1.5.\n- Amlodipine and atorvastatin continued.\n\nAll four items that arrived during the work are closed in your results inbox.",
